t was\\nthe largest remaining source of loss at capture, and most of what it dropped\\nis exactly what a downstream consumer needs: whether an agent was sandboxed\\nwhen it said something, what a file looked like between two commits, what a\\nsubagent actually did to produce its one-line answer.\\n\\nFour new kinds, and a rule for which is which:\\n\\n  session_state      a declaration that holds until restated — mode,\\n                     permission mode, sandbox/approval policy, model\\n                     settings, title, worktree relocation, codex\\n                     turn_context/session_meta/world_state\\n  activity           a point-in-time occurrence that is not a turn — hooks,\\n                     turn durations, token counts, task lifecycle, queue\\n                     operations, compaction, opencode step boundaries\\n  context_injection  material the harness put into the model's context that\\n                     nobody typed — Claude Code attachment lines\\n  file_snapshot      intermediate file state between commits\\n\\nAll four use one content shape: a *_type discriminator naming the source's\\nown type verbatim, the source's own field names beside it, and `blocks` in\\nthe ordinary [{type,text}] form wherever a record carries prose, so text\\nextraction needs no per-kind special case.\\n\\nThe skip lists are gone, except for codex's two event_msg payload types that\\ngenuinely duplicate a response_item. Keeping a skip list required a type to\\nbe both known and worthless, and almost nothing was. Removing a type from a\\nskip list would previously route it to the *unrecognized* branch — preserved,\\nbut drift-warned, which is wrong for a known upstream type and would have\\nmade the drift warning stop meaning \\\"the format moved\\\". Now the routing has a\\nthird destination instead.\\n\\nTwo structural additions make it work:\\n\\nConversationRef.parent makes a sub-conversation expressible. Claude Code\\nsidechains and opencode subagent sessions were both dropped entirely; each is\\nnow its own conversation pointing back at the session that spawned it, so\\n`show <subagent>` reads one in isolation and `show <session>` still matches\\nthe whole tree. opencode child sessions are only reachable via the parent's\\n`task` tool part metadata (`session list` returns top-level sessions only),\\nso capture walks them depth-first, cycle-guarded.\\n\\nEvidenceEvent.resolved holds what the ledger read at capture time from\\npointers the source only names — today, the sha256 and size of a Claude Code\\nfile-history backup. It is deliberately outside the identity subset: the\\nbacking cache is machine-local and prunable, so the same line resolves\\ndifferently elsewhere, and folding it into the id would duplicate every\\nsnapshot on a rescan after a prune. Digests, not bytes: the redaction stack\\nwalks content and raw.data, not this field.\\n\\nDisplay is separate from capture. log/show/conversations hide the four new\\nkinds behind --with-state, because a session restates its mode and tracked\\nfiles far more often than anyone speaks. Capture, export and sync are\\nunaffected.\\n\\nExisting ledgers upgrade: `cledger renormalize` now reconstructs non-turn\\nrecords from lines preserved as `unrecognized` before the adapters knew them,\\nwith the same id a live capture produces, so the two dedup. file_snapshot is\\nexcluded from that path on purpose — the cache its pointers name is long gone\\nby the time anyone runs renormalize, and an upgrade would permanently displace\\na preserved line with a digest-less record.\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_011yyjMrYMdcPPiKnPmUm8Hs\",\"messageHeadline\":\"Capture the whole record, not just the turns\",\"oid\":\"1100858ed17a7ee0b377097e93eafa24d3638f68\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-08-05T00:59:35Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"evintunador@gmail.com\",\"id\":\"MDQ6VXNlcjU3MjQwNzU4\",\"login\":\"evintunador\",\"name\":\"Evin Tunador\"},{\"email\":\"noreply@anthropic.com\",\"id\":\"MDQ6VXNlcjgxODQ3\",\"login\":\"claude\",\"name\":\"Claude Opus 5 (1M context)\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-08-05T00:59:35Z\",\"messageBody\":\"Two holes found by running a real session against the branch rather than\\nreplaying saved transcripts. Both are places where the conversion was already\\ncorrect and nothing ever fed it the bytes.\\n\\nSubagent transcripts were never read. Newer Claude Code writes a subagent to\\n<session>/subagents/agent-<id>.jsonl — a sibling file, mentioned nowhere in\\nthe session transcript and not named by the hook payload, which carries only\\nthe main transcript_path. Sidechain conversion worked fine; no code path ever\\nopened the file. Capture now walks the subagents directory depth-first,\\ncycle-guarded, since a subagent can spawn its own. (Older versions inlined the\\nsame content as isSidechain lines; both layouts convert identically, because\\nthe lines carry the same fields.)\\n\\nA session cannot capture its own last lines. The hook reads to end-of-file,\\nthen Claude Code writes the closing bookkeeping of the final turn — including\\nthe session's most complete file-history-snapshot, the one naming every\\ntracked file's backup. No further hook fires, so those lines sat behind a\\ncursor nothing would advance again. Observed live: a cursor resting at line 40\\nof a 50-line transcript, with the only snapshot naming a real backup at line\\n49. The hook now sweeps other transcripts in the same project whose file grew\\npast the size recorded with their cursor, so the next session in a repo closes\\nthe previous one's gap. Cursors gained that size field; an older cursor\\nwithout one reads as 0, re-reads once, and dedups.\\n\\nMeasured on the session that found both: 21 events captured before, 40 after,\\nwith the subagent conversation present and file digests resolving.\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_011yyjMrYMdcPPiKnPmUm8Hs\",\"messageHeadline\":\"claude-code: capture subagent transcripts and stranded transcript tails\",\"oid\":\"22af6246a95360b0310727583470b47b6dfff17d\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-08-05T02:24:46Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"evintunador@gmail.com\",\"id\":\"MDQ6VXNlcjU3MjQwNzU4\",\"login\":\"evintunador\",\"name\":\"Evin Tunador\"},{\"email\":\"noreply@anthropic.com\",\"id\":\"MDQ6VXNlcjgxODQ3\",\"login\":\"claude\",\"name\":\"Claude Opus 5 (1M context)\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-08-05T02:24:46Z\",\"messageBody\":\"Standalone `reasoning` response_items have been preserved opaquely since\\n0.10.0 — that is what lets a consumer replay a codex conversation back through\\nthe provider that encrypted it, across accounts. The `encrypted_content`\\nblocks embedded inside `agent_message` payloads were named as a scope cut in\\nthat same release and never picked up, so inter-agent sessions stayed the one\\nplace capture destroyed provider-withheld material rather than sealing it.\\n\\nNot a corner case: 126 of 165 `agent_message` response_items in the local\\ncorpus carry encrypted blocks, and inter-agent traffic is exactly the sort of\\nsession whose reasoning cannot be reconstructed from the visible turns.\\n\\nAn inter-agent message now becomes two events at the same seq — the visible\\nturn, and a sealed `reasoning`-kind sibling whose raw.data is the line reduced\\nto its encrypted blocks. The pairing, and the reason distinct kinds keep the\\nids from colliding, are the same as a reasoning item with a populated summary.\\nThe visible turn is unchanged: it still keeps a bare\\n{type: \\\"encrypted_content\\\"} marker where a sealed block was, which is what\\nmakes the split visible rather than silent.\\n\\nThe redaction stack already exempts `.../encrypted_content` on reasoning-kind\\nevents, so pattern-matching cannot corrupt a blob it cannot read; the test\\nasserts the stored blob byte-for-byte, which pins that exemption too.\\n\\nVerified against a real rollout: all 62 encrypted agent_message items\\npreserved, blobs intact at 6-8KB each.\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_011yyjMrYMdcPPiKnPmUm8Hs\",\"messageHeadline\":\"codex: preserve inter-agent ciphertext instead of dropping it\",\"oid\":\"f14e4a66e3275ee55ee9c85aff22c1925a24a4d9\"}],\"deletions\":260,\"headRefName\":\"worktree-capture-everything\",\"isDraft\":true,\"number\":4,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"Capture the whole record, not just the turns\"}"},"type":"user","userType":"external","uuid":"2a07ff49-53bb-4f37-8e25-0a3eebfcc765","version":"2.1.222"},"format":"claude-code-jsonl/1"},"recorded_at":"2026-08-06T00:30:20.285Z","schema":"conversation-ledger/v1"}
